      "message": "\u003e Here we are matching:\n\u003e \n\u003e * leading non-zero digit (for example PS first number)\n\u003e * followed by any ocurrence of digits, the characters ., ^, or + (for example from the second digit of a PS, or ., ^, or +)\n\nNo, in Java regex the `^` indicates a negative match. So `^+` mean _everything but NOT `+`_\n\n\u003e \n\u003e Questions:\n\u003e * is this supposed to match PS ranges? 1..2\n\nYes, it does because it includes the `.` as well.\n\n\u003e * which case are `^` and `+` trying to match?\n\nSee above.\n\n\u003e If they are a real case we should probably add a test case for it. It seems we are matching cases like `/123456/1+2` or `/123456/1^2`. Is this intended?\n\nNo, I believe you read it wrongly :-)",
